Good catch 👍 — that error means **`waydroid` isn’t in Manjaro’s default repositories**.
Let’s fix it 👇

---

### 🟢 Step 1 — Enable AUR (if not already)

Waydroid is available in the **AUR (Arch User Repository)**, not the default Manjaro repos.
So you need an AUR helper like `yay` or `pamac`.

#### If you already have `yay`:

You can directly install Waydroid:

```bash
yay -S waydroid
```

#### If you don’t have `yay` yet:

Install it:

```bash
sudo pacman -S --needed base-devel git
git clone https://aur.archlinux.org/yay.git
cd yay
makepkg -si
```

Then run:

```bash
yay -S waydroid
```

---

### 🟢 Step 2 — Enable and start the Waydroid container

Once installed, set up the service:

```bash
sudo systemctl enable --now waydroid-container
```

Then initialize the Android image:

```bash
sudo waydroid init
```

---

### 🟢 Step 3 — Start session and launch GUI

```bash
waydroid session start
waydroid show-full-ui
```

You’ll now see the Android interface inside a window on your Manjaro desktop.
From there, open the **browser inside Waydroid**, go to [whatsapp.com/android](https://www.whatsapp.com/android), and install the APK.

---

### ✅ Optional — Install APK directly

If you downloaded `WhatsApp.apk` to your Linux system:

```bash
waydroid app install ~/Downloads/WhatsApp.apk
```

Then launch:

```bash
waydroid app launch com.whatsapp
```

---

### 🔊 Calls support

Voice and video calls **work** in Waydroid (because it runs a real Android environment), provided:

* Your **microphone** and **camera** are available to Waydroid
* You allowed permissions inside Android Settings → Apps → WhatsApp

